# ffi/logs/rust/stage3-unknown-fields.log
# W3: the unknown-field bag, built as an ARM and priced. ABI v1 open decision 11.
# 2026-09-18
#
# CONFIGURATION (R7)
#   machine        4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.80GHz, 15 GB, Ubuntu 24.04.4, Linux 6.18.44 x86_64,
#                  container, NOT an isolated host. No CPU pinning, no governor control.
#   rust           rustc/cargo 1.94.1, profile release (opt-level 3, lto off, PIE)
#   MSRV declared  1.88. NOT VERIFIED: no 1.88 toolchain exists in this container.
#   incumbent      prost 0.14.4, carried as the in-process control on every timed row
#   LINKAGE        cdylib (libak_core.so), dynamic linker. Not a static link.
#   guard          on
#   content        ASCII.
#   class          M1 (P1.1-P1.3), M2 (P2.1-P2.5) and M3 (P3.1) are all REAL messages.
#
# ============================================================================
# READ THIS FIRST: THE PUBLISHED M1/M2 RATIOS DO NOT REPRODUCE TODAY, AND IT IS
# NOT THIS ARM'S DOING
# ============================================================================
#   Before trusting anything below against the published table, note that the published
#   table no longer reproduces on this container AT THE COMMIT IT WAS TAKEN FROM. Measured
#   in two fresh worktrees today, same machine, same session:
#
#     P1.2 encode, / prost        published        HEAD (4afffd9b)   7fb30be5
#       armonik                   0.967 - 1.032    1.150             1.146
#       core-native               0.425 - 0.438    0.564             0.544
#       core-ffi-rust             0.706 - 0.716    1.011             0.992
#     P1.3 decode, core-ffi-rust  1.313 - 1.393    1.725             -
#
#   Every arm moved the same way, including `armonik`, which is prost's own codec over the
#   facade types and touches nothing this slice has changed since. A bisect to `7fb30be5`
#   -- before the zeroed-group work, before this one -- shows the same drift, so it is not
#   a regression introduced by either. The most likely cause is the container: it is shared,
#   unpinned, and the ratios were taken on a different day.
#
#   **What follows from it for this log**: every number below is a delta formed INSIDE ONE
#   PROCESS between two arms that ran in the same interleaved rounds, with prost as the
#   control in that same process. Those deltas are what this log reports. The absolute
#   ratios to prost are printed for reproducibility and must NOT be read against the
#   published M1/M2 table.
#
# ============================================================================
# THE GATING QUESTION, ANSWERED FROM THE GENERATOR'S OWN PREDICATE
# ============================================================================
#   ABI v1 7.2: a repeated field may be handed over as a run if and only if its element type
#   contains no repeated and no map field, transitively. If the bag were modelled as a
#   REPEATED thing, every element type in the schema would acquire a repeated field.
#
#   `gen/unknown_predicate.py` puts a bag into an in-memory copy of the schema under each
#   modelling and runs `emit/shapes.py:is_leaf` -- the predicate the generator itself uses,
#   imported and not re-derived (R1) -- over the result. Section 1 below is its output:
#
#     leaf messages today                 9 of 19
#     leaf messages, a REPEATED bag       0 of 19     <- every element type stops batching
#     leaf messages, ONE bytes blob       9 of 19     <- no message changes
#
#   A repeated bag is refused on structure alone, before any measurement: it would cost
#   `ResultRaw`'s batched run, which is what turns 1000 rows into 9 crossings. Everything
#   below is built on the one-blob model and on nothing else.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHAT IS BUILT
# ============================================================================
#   encode   one slot per group (`ak_ufix_*`), appended verbatim by the codec after the
#            known fields. A nested message gets its OWN bag, so a child slot is the child's
#            `u` group. Four entry points exist per root -- total fill and zeroed, each with
#            and without the bag -- because decision 9 interacts: the bag adds a slot to
#            every group, so the total fill and the per-chunk memset both grow.
#
#   THE SLOT IS TWO WORDS, NOT THREE, and this is a deliberate choice worth stating because
#   a reader will otherwise wonder whether the bag was left on a transcoding path. Every
#   other blob slot carries a transcoder pointer because the host's representation may
#   differ from the wire's. The bag's cannot: it IS wire bytes a decoder captured. With
#   decision 3 settled, `ak_tc_bytes` and `ak_tc_utf8_trusted` are already the same memcpy,
#   so a transcoder here would be a pointer whose only legal value is the identity -- dead
#   weight on every group of every message and an invitation to set it wrong. Emptiness is
#   `len == 0`, which is the test ABI v1 section 8's direct-argument path already uses, so
#   this is not a new convention. The saving is 8 bytes per group per message, which is
#   directly the quantity decisions 9 and 11 interact through.
#
#   decode   unknown runs captured as spans into the buffer the host handed in and delivered
#            as a SIDE RUN keyed by token -- `ak_uspan { token, off, len }` -- not as a slot
#            in the element group. So the core copies nothing, allocates nothing, and the
#            element group is not touched at all, which is why the predicate never even sees
#            the decode half. The host materialises the bytes if it intends to re-encode,
#            because that buffer may be recycled.
#
#   host     `unknown_fields: Vec<u8>` on every facade type. NOT a schema field: nothing
#            tags it, `prost_impl.rs` does not write it and `core_native.rs` does not read
#            it, so every existing arm is unaffected and byte identity on the payload set is
#            unchanged (section 2).
#
# ============================================================================
# THE CORRECTNESS PROPERTY, STATED PRECISELY, BECAUSE IT IS DELIBERATELY RELAXED
# ============================================================================
#   The bag is APPENDED AT THE END. No merge by tag, by instruction. That splits the
#   round-trip property in two and this log checks both separately:
#
#   1. THE BAG'S BYTES ARE PRESERVED EXACTLY. Still a byte property, still checkable: the
#      retained blob against the unknown runs of the input. Column `bag == input`.
#   2. THE MESSAGE'S BYTE LAYOUT IS NOT, when an unknown tag sits numerically between two
#      known ones. Canonical form ascends by tag and the bag goes last, so decode-then-
#      re-encode is NOT byte-identical to the input for that case. Column `layout ident`,
#      with `expected` saying which vectors that applies to. The gate is agreement with the
#      expectation, not identity: a vector that became identical when it should not have
#      would mean the bag had silently been merged.
#   3. SO THAT CASE IS VALIDATED SEMANTICALLY: decode, re-encode, decode again, compare
#      values -- which includes the retained blob, since it is a field of the value.
#      Column `semantic RT`.
#
#   A PROPERTY THE MIGRATION NOTE NEEDS, not a defect: a message round-tripped through the
#   core is no longer byte-comparable with the same message round-tripped through
#   protobuf-java, whose `UnknownFieldSet` writes in field-number order. FRESH ENCODES FROM
#   A VALUE ARE UNAFFECTED, so `ffi/schema/generated/manifest.json` and everything resting
#   on it are untouched -- section 2 is the evidence.
#
# ============================================================================
# MEASUREMENT HAZARDS (R9)
# ============================================================================
#   1. The drift above. Read deltas, not absolutes.
#   2. Container, shared vCPUs, no pinning; spread is printed per row and exceeds 50 percent
#      on some rows. Three runs are printed.
#   3. The empty-bag decode cost is one `let s0 = d.pos;` per field in every decode loop and
#      one null test on a branch the existing payload set never takes. It is a difference of
#      two large numbers and lands inside the spread on most rows; treat a sub-nanosecond
#      per-element figure as "not measurable here", not as a value.
#   4. The bag's cost to the HOST's data model -- 24 bytes per message instance for a
#      `Vec<u8>` -- is carried by every arm in this build, including the ones that ignore
#      the bag. It is therefore NOT in the bag-versus-no-bag deltas below, which compare two
#      arms over the same objects. It is real and it is unmeasured here; separating it would
#      need two facade type sets in one process, which this slice does not have.
#
# ============================================================================
# WHAT THIS LOG SAYS, BEFORE THE NUMBERS
# ============================================================================
#   1. THE BAG DOES NOT COST THE BATCHING. As one opaque `bytes` blob it changes the
#      leafness of no message; as a repeated field it would take the schema from 9 leaf
#      messages to 0. Structure, from the generator's own predicate, before any timing.
#   2. THE EMPTY BAG IS FREE ON DECODE and NOT free on encode. Decode, capture on against
#      capture off: 0.978 to 1.002, per-element deltas straddling zero on every payload --
#      not measurable here. Encode, bag against no bag, same fill, same process:
#        P1.2  total fill +2.7..+4.7%   zeroed +2.5..+3.4%
#        P1.3  total fill +7.8..+12.2%  zeroed +0.3..+0.7%
#        P2.2  total fill +0.7..+1.7%   zeroed +3.9..+9.4%
#        P2.5  total fill +4.2..+4.6%   zeroed +2.0..+8.4%
#      In production the bag is ALWAYS empty -- ArmoniK ships both sides from one release --
#      so this is the number decision 11 is actually deciding on, and it is one to five
#      percent of an encode on the shapes that move, with an outlier at twelve.
#   3. AND THE DECISION-9 INTERACTION IS REAL AND GOES BOTH WAYS. On P1.3, where the fill IS
#      the cost, the bag costs 8 to 12 percent under the total fill and is FREE under the
#      zeroed variant -- the memset absorbs the extra slot, the unconditional stores do not.
#      On P2.2 it is the other way round: 0.7 to 1.7 percent under the total fill and 3.9 to
#      9.4 under the zeroed one. Neither fill dominates, and a slice that priced the bag
#      under one fill alone would have got the sign of the interaction wrong on half the
#      payloads.
#   4. THE BAG'S BYTES ROUND-TRIP EXACTLY; THE MESSAGE'S LAYOUT DOES NOT when an unknown tag
#      sits between two known ones. Both are checked separately and the second is validated
#      semantically, per the instruction to append rather than merge.
#   5. WHAT IS STILL NOT PRICED: the 24 bytes a `Vec<u8>` adds to every message instance in
#      the host's own data model. Every arm in this build carries it, so it cancels in the
#      deltas above. It is real, it lands on hosts that never use the bag, and separating it
#      would need two facade type sets in one process.
#
