# =============================================================================
# SUMMARY, DERIVED FROM THE THREE RUNS ABOVE AND FROM NOTHING ELSE
# =============================================================================
#
# THE EMPTY-BAG COST. Every figure is a delta between two arms that ran in the same
# interleaved rounds of the same process, with prost as the control in that process.
# min .. max over the three runs. Positive = the bag costs.
#
# payload  bag / total fill         bag / zeroed             capture / no capture
# P1.2     1.027-1.047  +4.3..+7.3  1.025-1.034  +3.9..+5.3  1.000-1.002  -0.1..+1.2
# P1.3     1.078-1.122  +1.9..+2.9  1.003-1.007  +0.0..+0.1  0.978-1.001  -1.3..+0.1
# P2.2     1.007-1.017  +7.7..+18.8 1.039-1.094 +42.8..+102  0.992-1.000 -31.6..-0.2
# P2.5     1.042-1.046  +1.4..+1.6  1.020-1.084  +0.7..+2.9  0.993-0.999  -1.0..-0.1
#
#   ns figures are per element. P2.2's are large in absolute terms because a `TaskDetailed`
#   is a 27-field group that costs ten crossings; as a FRACTION it is the cheapest row for
#   the total fill and the dearest for the zeroed one.
#
#   The decode column is a null result and should be read as one: the per-element deltas
#   straddle zero on three of four payloads and the ratios sit inside the run-to-run spread.
#   That is what the design predicts -- the capture adds one `let s0 = d.pos;` per field and
#   a null test on the default arm, which the existing payload set never takes -- but the
#   measurement does not distinguish it from zero, and saying so is not the same as saying
#   it is zero.
#
# THE CORRECTNESS TABLE, identical in all three runs:
#
#   vector                                       bag==input  layout ident  expected  semantic RT
#   all four wire types, AFTER every known field  yes         yes           yes       yes
#   one unknown BEFORE every known field          yes         no            no        yes
#   an unknown tag BETWEEN two known tags         yes         no            no        yes
#   two elements, each with its own unknown field yes         yes           yes       yes
#
#   So: nothing is ever lost, and the layout is reproduced exactly when the unknown fields
#   were already last -- which is the common forward-compatibility case, a newer sender
#   appending fields. It is not reproduced when an unknown tag is interleaved, by design.
#
# THE POSITIVE CONTROL, in every run: the DEFAULT decode retains 0 bytes and its re-encode
# is 6 B against 62 B in. An arm that silently dropped the bag would pass every byte-identity
# check in section 2 and measure identically, which is exactly the failure mode the
# zeroed-group controls were built for; this is the same check for this arm.
#
# WHAT IS NOT MEASURED
#   - The non-empty bag's THROUGHPUT. The vectors prove the property on messages of tens of
#     bytes; nothing here encodes a large payload whose elements all carry unknown fields,
#     so "what retention costs when it is actually retaining" is unpriced.
#   - The host data-model cost (24 bytes per message instance). See hazard 4.
#   - A oneof's message member gets no bag: `dec_*_fix` is handed a null capture buffer
#     there, because attributing those runs would need a bag on the member rather than on
#     the enclosing message. Refused rather than emitted wrong.
#   - The inner slots of a NON-leaf element (M2's repeated fields inside `TaskDetailed`)
#     have no unknown callback: the vtable carries `unk_<slot>` for top-level slots only.
#   - Decode-side crossings: the capture delivers spans as a run, so a message with unknown
#     fields costs extra reverse crossings per chunk. The counting build was not extended to
#     count them, because the payload set has no unknown fields to count.
#   - Merge-by-tag. Not built and not priced, by instruction.
